2|0@AskReddit^1440434524#cue1ehy:PotatoManPerson$:Never. Unless you live in a Gaeltacht region (an area where Irish is the main language - which there aren't that much of) or go to school where you get taught it, you never use it. 
1|0@AskReddit^1440434646#cue1h93:Pays_in_snakes$:Is there not even any commonly-used gaelic slang? 
2|0@AskReddit^1440436398#cue2m6g:EmzieM$:It's common to say small phrases. Craic is technically an Irish word. It's not unusual to say 'Aon scéal?' instead of 'Any news?' or to say you're a 'citóg' instead of left handed. People often use simple Irish nouns in everyday language like bainne (milk), tae (tea), leaba (bed) etc - things you would remember easily from school. We also tend to say things like 'as gaeilge' (in Irish) when speaking English and referring to something said in Irish and people also might say they have a 'grá' (love) for something. We also impose Irish habits onto English words. Anything ending in '-een' in Irish usually means 'small' so we say things like 'biteen' meaning 'a small bit' or 'peteen' meaning 'little pet' (pet is a term of endearment, like sweetie). I should note though, I'm originally from a very rural background and now live in the West where Irish is spoken a lot. People from cities or the East might not hear as much Irish. 
1|0@AskReddit^1440437268#cue36oz:PotatoManPerson$:Yeah, I'm from Louth and honestly, the only Irish word I use is Craic. But the west is a whole different story
1|0@AskReddit^1440431391#cudzevi:holytriplem$:[This](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eyll-bBZzyk) might answer your question (tl;dr, not a lot)
1|0@AskReddit^1440461326#cueid29:saolsaor$:The usage of Irish day-to-day really depends on where in the country you are! I live in a very rural parish, and my parents were raised in rural parishes too, so I would use it on a day-to-day basis. Nothing more than the basics, though. My knowledge of Irish isn't good enough to have a full blown debate on current issues, but myself and my parents do say quite a few things in Irish instead of English. That being said, none of my siblings would ever speak in Irish day-to-day, so it depends on your love of the language too. Even though I wasn't the best at Irish in school, I still love it and would love it if it was spoken as well as English in the country, but my siblings hated the language and haven't spoken a word of it since leaving school.
