2|0@AskReddit^1440433373#cue0nwu:imeatingpbnj$:we went to this adorable little town that was dominated by one tourist attraction. so there were squillions of tourists there, all just there for the day or staying maximum one night. we stayed for four. everybody we passed on the street, who actually lived there, was *so nice*. waving, greeting, smiling, just saying hello as we walked by. like we were the new people in town. along with 500 other new people who would be gone by the next morning. it was incredible just how welcomed we felt, even though it must be so hard on the town to have its identity wrapped up in tourists who don't even stay a full day. i just found that odd, especially since everybody was so friendly, but not interested in making friends. completely silent if you sat down next to them in the bar, sly teasing remarks about our accents or weight. i mean it makes sense. i saw the tourism industry as being incredibly draining, maybe not on the irish economy, but definitely on the irish people. do you agree, OP?
