1|0@AskReddit^1440434031#cue138z:BoxBopChallenge$:How is Irish history taught to Irish students? In the US for example, there's a heavy emphasis on the potato famine, immigration, and hostility toward the UK.
5|0@AskReddit^1440435740#cue270z:PrahaPrincess$:It's not the emphasis; we learn a lot of Ancient/European/World History too. The famine is dealt with at primary (elementary) school level, and it's very basic really; more of a social history rather than political/economical. Immigration is still a fact of life, so it's not considered a 'historical' issue, but an on-going one. When we study the War of Independence, England are ''the bad guys'', but it's a fairly balanced account of what happened. IMO, as a history teacher, we could and should focus more on Irish history. The course is too broad, so nothing gets too much time or detail.
2|0@AskReddit^1440435993#cue2cti:PotatoManPerson$:What you say sounds very much alike what we learn, we probably go more in depth though. As well as this, we learn about the times of when the Vikings invaded Ireland, when Christianity was brought to Ireland and we learn about our Paganism beforehand. Things like that. We actually have a very interesting culture, even though a lot of it is religious and I'm am atheist haha.
1|0@AskReddit^1440468724#cuemv28:lolotron$:Preyttty much the same. More in depth stuff on politics and economics but it depends. I'm glad you're taught that stuff. (Maybe not the resentment stuff but to say it's not there at all would be a bit of a lie)
