1|0@AskReddit^1440449443#cueb8pn:holytriplem$:To be fair though, there was huge opposition to the Iraq War even in the UK. Part of the reason why Tony Blair is so despised isn't just that he invaded Iraq in the first place, but that he did it in spite of almost the entire population of the UK opposing him. Before the invasion, there was a public demonstration to attempt to stop the invasion from taking place and around 750000 people turned up. So there would have been a lot of anti-American sentiment in the UK too from that period, since Blair was seen as the poodle of the US. The anti-American sentiment has definitely died down since Obama took power.
2|0@AskReddit^1440497481#cuex0eh:tedstevens1923$:It's because we're supposed to be neutral. It's in the constitution and should require a national referendum to break that neutrality and the government has ignored that by allowing troop and rendition flights through shannon airport.
1|0@AskReddit^1440469112#cuen3dn:lolotron$:It's true, we're very neutral. We're proud of our neutral status during ww2. However, the Irish know that we're were 'allies neutral' meaning we allowed stopovers for raf refuels and American long distance squadrons. Downed german pilots would have been escorted to British pow camps whereas allied soldiers would have been helped back home. Basically, from this, we don't want anything to do with wars, especially ones that have nothing to do with us and are waged for perhaps questionable reasons, but that's a completely different debate. By the way, I personally have huge respect for people who go for military service, especially with the priority role as peacekeepers that modern armies have today. Glad you made it home safe ;)
