2|0@AskReddit^1440431665#cudzl21:holytriplem$:I'm curious as to how Irish people have managed to transform from being one of the most socially conservative countries in Europe to a progressive society like any other in Europe in the space of 20-30 years? (for instance, voting against legalising divorce in the 80s but voting for legalising gay marriage this year)? Would you still say that Ireland is a religious society, and how much power does the Catholic Church still have in Ireland? Also, how do you feel about English people?
5|0@AskReddit^1440433950#cue11dp:EmzieM$:I can only really offer my own opinions but here it goes: Education is probably how we became so progressive so quickly. Education is largely free/very affordable in Ireland and is emphasised as being very important in our culture. As a result, we are now [one of those most] (http://www.thejournal.ie/ireland-educated-europe-graduates-third-level-2232866-Jul2015/) if not [the most educated country] (http://www.voltedge.ie/human-resources-news/did-you-know-that-ireland-is-the-most-educated-countries-in-eu/) in Europe. We're also culturally very inclined towards travel and living abroad due to a strong history of emigration but when our economy picked up those people who had left started coming home en masse meaning that we have a high proportion of people who are well educated and who have lived abroad/traveled extensively, which opens them up to new cultures and ways of thinking. Also, being English speakers, it is easier for us to learn about progressive ideas from America, Canada, the UK, Australia etc without language barriers. The massive decline of the Catholic Church also had a huge impact. This occurred, in my opinion, due to a combination of us becoming more educated and being exposed to more international influences and also due to a [series of abuse scandals] (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Catholic_Church_sexual_abuse_scandal_in_Ireland). In 2011, only 18% of Irish people went to mass regularly. Twenty years earlier, that figure was 90%. It was a *dramatic* decline ([source] (http://thecircular.org/the-decline-of-catholicism-in-ireland/) ). By and large, we are not a religious country anymore. The older generation would still be quite conservative but it is very rare to find a religious young person and even if you do they are unlikely to follow any dogmas and are usually fine with homosexuality, contraception, divorce etc etc. We tend to use 'Irish Catholic' as a cultural/ethnic identifier rather than a description of our actual beliefs. The anti abortion laws are one of the last leftover vice grip that the church has on our government but that will hopefully be removed in the next 5 - 10 years. The vast, vast majority of Irish people have no problem whatsoever with English people and have many English friends and are happy to have them live, work and travel here and for us to do the same in England. There are people who might make comments about English people in general or bring up politics but it's very rare to find someone with any actual hatred. Edit: typos and details 
1|0@AskReddit^1440499932#cuexlb6:tiddlypeeps$:My opinion on this is that religion just pushed the country too far in too short a space of time. We went from a third world country to economically prosperous in a very short space of time (thanks mostly to the EU), which brought education and mass media to most homes. This brought the very recent atrocities of religious institutes to light in a very big way. The sex abuse scandal with the catholic church, the magdaline laundries run by the catholic church and the troubles up north done in the name of the catholic church and the church of Ireland. A lot of negativity was revealed about religious institutions in a very short space of time. This made a lot of people want to distance themselves from it. In 20/30 years we have gone from a place where a son becoming a priest would have been a thing to brag about to a place when a son becoming a priest would be a thing to keep to yourself unless someone asked because it's considered extremely unusual maybe even a little embarrassing. 
1|0@AskReddit^1440468422#cuemocc:lolotron$:Because it's a quick transition, there are still many citizens with the mindset of a *much* more convenient conservative time
