1|0@AskReddit^1440431845#cudzp4x:LLment$:What's the deal with Northern Ireland? And the IRA
-2|0@AskReddit^1440435402#cue1yz8:PotatoManPerson$:Ah, ask the wrong person this question and you will be talking to them for hours. Basically, England ruled Ireland for hundreds if years. We had a war of independence in the first half of the 1900s. This resulted in the north of Ireland still being ruled by England but the rest becoming a republic. Many didn't like this decision, and wanted all of Ireland to be free. The Protestants from England stayed in the north, the Catholics from Ireland were mainly in the south. There was lots of tension between them and this started the "Troubles". The IRA had been around since the war of Independence, and they basically want all of Ireland to be free from England. It stands for the Irish Republican Army. They would use violence to get what they wanted - a lot of bombs. Anyway, this all ended with the Good Friday Agreement in the 80s. The Troubles ended, and a lot of the tension between England and Ireland and the North and the South diffused. It's not a problem anymore for a southerner to go to the north or vice versa.
